In article <1995Mar2.0...@cs.cornell.edu>,
Alan Kwan <kw...@cs.cornell.edu> wrote:
// A collectable trading-card game can either be based
// on card-resources (as MtG is), or based heavily on
// some other game resources (as Jyhad tends to be).
// Having some cards powerfully outweighting others
// would shift a game towards the former. Such a
// game would certainly sell like rabbits, because
// players /have to/ fish out $$$ so as to "build"
// their resources ...

Yes, but most of the cards you've set out as spoilers are _common_ cards.
This makes them much easier to get. In magic, most of the common cards
are unusable in favour of uncommon or rare cards.

// The problem with over-powerful cards is exactly that everyone
// is putting those cards in their decks (if they have them)
// regardless of their overall deck strategy. Any deck
// without a Minion Tap or 2 (or 4) would not do as well ...

Very poor example. Most people I've seen around here with successful
decks occasionally have maybe 1 minion tap (never mind the accepted local
maximum of 2 of any). Blood dolls tend to be a more sound choice,
but you have to decide depending on the deck if you want the quick
grab, or a slow grab that can also be used to heal the vampire
(Mariel really appreciates it from time-to-time). More sound examples
are wwef, KRCG, or sport bikes. However, saying a deck wouldn't do
as well without 2 (or especially 4) minion taps is silly, I'd have
to remove a more useful blood card like a short term, or worse
yet thin out my deck theme cards -- and for a card that gives
me a one shot suck! I'll play my current set a blood cards which
has blood dolls which can suck blood to me from all kinds of
continuous blood sources instead, it may be slow, but I've see
5 blood easily syphoned in a turn without a minion tap...

--
Brent Ross // "Skuld is nicer than // "Skuld is nicer than
bwr...@uwaterloo.ca // Medea." -- mlvanbie // mead." -- mtompset

In article <1995Mar1.0...@cs.cornell.edu>,
Alan Kwan <kw...@cs.cornell.edu> wrote:
// Think about WwEF. Some people use more than 10.

Ah, I see what your problem is... the people you play with are on
serious drugs. :) Really, you should just get together and agree
on some reasonable house rules (around here 2 of any card, elsewhere
it seems to be 4... Misdirection is baned from friendly now, other
cards like Monicle just give you a bad rep). If someone refuses
to play within the house rules, don't play with them.

// [talk of raising wwef's cost]
// On a side note, I think in Revised the card should
// only be useable if you are playing another reaction or
// are actually blocking (a blockable action at
// enough intercept). In other words, no dumping
// the card on a bleed across the table or on a
// stealthed action without risking combat.

Once you limit the number of wwef's you can have in a deck, anyone who
wastes a wwef like this will be considered an idiot. Wwef is the best
card in the game, I personally don't mind an uping of the cost to 1,
but I would really like it left just the way it is (ie. I don't find in
unbalancing, it's a _common_, _defensive_ card not a _club my opponent
over the head for nothing_ card). The reason I like wwef is that
because its a nice free card, any tapped vampire can use it. This
means that the game actually moves at a faster pace... because people
can do more actions in a turn and leave fewer untapped blockers. Some
of the most interesting strategy is trying to get your opponents to
waste wwef's on items you aren't concerned about. Once someone has
played all wwef's in their deck, they lose a good about of their
ability to bluff having them (which is the best part of the game --
seeing how far you can bluff your opponents).

// and the Animalism untaps. The cost of "Do not // Replace until end
of Combat" for a /reaction/ // (not combat) card simply outweights the
// requirement of a discipline (WwEF is also // more flexible than the
Animalism untaps).

Don't forget it doesn't untap the vampire! If you use one and the
opponent slips through with obfuscate, your card is gone, part of your
bluff is gone, you've effectivly gained nothing, and your prey might
decide you probably don't have another and jump down your throat (which
may or may not be a problem depending on if you have other surprises).

// Another one is Misdirection. If it costs 1+X,
// people will no doubt still use it. If it costs
// 2+X, some people may still use it, but some will
// be taking it out of their decks, If it costs
// 2X, then I bet people will shake their heads and
// take it out. So 2X is no doubt too expensive,
// but X is simply too cheap. Because there are
// Dominate bleeds and the like, 2+X is not
// entirely unreasonable.

Misdirection should probably be removed as a spoiler, it adds no
stategic elements to the game and creates coin-flip type wins more
times than not. Making it 2+X is not good enough in my books, you can
still tap 4 minions and kill your opponent before you actually have to
_pay_ for the victory point. 2X is starting to become a reasonable
card. The basic problem with misdirection is that it is far too
similar to berzerk as a spoiler (ie. no one should be forced to take
multiple 4+ bleeds just because they don't have a handful of wwefs or a
sudden reversal -- basically the game balance problem is that
multi-card combos are fairly balanced by deck construction rules since
it takes work and luck to put them together, however a one card spoiler
which requires the opponent to effectivly "combo" it to survive is
really gross). The biggest problem is that where wwef can speed a game
up, having misdirections really slows it down. People start playing
really conservativly, keeping more blood in their pool, not using their
vampires... all because of an insane fear that if my predetor has a
misdirection and I make it easy, I'll be plowed out of the game.

// A card less often talked about is Majesty.
// Depending on deck strategy, some players
// put 12 or more in their decks.

I've already mentioned that this type of serious drugs is bad, real
bad. :) Don't let me start talking about what I could do with 12
conditionings in a deck.

// A Strike:

// Combat Ends is many things in one. It counters
// all strikes, all additional strikes, and all
// presses.

It only actually "counters" a few presses (things like trap), and
never actually counters additional strikes unless both players
have them, or the celerity player is an idiot and plays his
blurs to soon.

// And if that is not enough, the

// Superior version allows you to pay 1 blood
// to untap.

One blood seems fairly standard for an untap (notice that wwef doesn't
untap and is free). Yes, this is powerful, but animalism also has
untaps on common cards, and Rat's Warning has it at both skill levels
(which is fine since it can only be used defensively).

// Imagine that the card costs 1 blood to play.

// There are so many combat situations in which
// the Combat Ends is easily worth /more than/ the 1 blood:
// the .44, a Brujah with Blur and 3 Undead Strengths,

(1) the .44 -- bringing out your own is better in general a Majesty
is a one shot, a .44 allows you to ping vampires
without long range attack capability without taking
damage many times... not to mention returning firepower
against other guns. Majesty is better when your really
concerned about what your opponent is going to do to
your vampire.

(2) the Brujah -- if he drops all 4 cards on the table at once and plays
them he deserves to lose the damage. You should
always wait to see your opponent's strike before
adding additional strikes or using things like
Wolf Claws. That way the worst this Brujah should
lose is 1 Undead Strength from declaring his strike.
Next time he'll probably be happy knowing that the
opponent has already used a majesty and he'll
be able to to the pumped hand/blur combo. Don't
rush through Jyhad combats, always get the order
down... this is much more important here than in
Magic.

// reasonable. Opponent used 1 action to Bum's

// Rush you, so you have to spend 1 blood
// to counter it. Remember that opponent has
// to build an entire combat deck to support it
// (unlike WwEF, which just goes in about any deck);
// you only need a fraction of your deck for
// those Majesty's.

Yes, but Bum's Rush doesn't require _any_ disipline. Arguing that
the defense should cost as much as the offense is absurd, it's well
shown in many games already that if defense is cheaper people will
actually: (a) play it and not build grossly offensive heavy decks
(sounds a lot like Magic, eh?), (b) have more fun since a solid
defense is more fun to try and take down than just running wild
all over your opponent.

// Many combat cards cost blood; I'm surprised

// why such a useful card as Majesty doesn't.

Many is an overstatement... "some" is a better word.

// Making it cost 1 blood is not going to screw

// the Ventrue's voting deck with Majesty:
// it just helps to balance the cards out.

Yes, but this card doesn't need to be fixed, it's not broke.

// (Please, no flames against an argument

// on restricting Majesty. That is not the
// argument here. I'm not talking about restricting
// Majesty, I'm talking about making it cost 1 blood.)

No flames intended, just pointing out that this isn't needed.

// Majesty is also one of those highly-demanded cards.

Not a really good method of finding cards that are unbalanced (but
perhaps a good filter to see which ones to look at).

// those that can give a lot of pool in combos,

// tend to be in great demand. Some ways should be
// thought of to limit the use of these cards.

Of course, then you wind up not being able to heal yourself at

all. Besides, once you limit all cards to a maximum of 2 each
you find that these combos don't come up much (if you want combos
you have to cut your deck close to 40 cards and then your deck
runs short of cards that do anything else... or just runs out of
cards in larger games).

// Another post of mine contained some examples,
// such as making Minion Tap cost 1 pool (in the least),
// limiting Giant's Blood to giving 3 blood, and making

Giant's Blood already has a decent limitation... it can only be
used once in a game. So you have to get one and play it before your
opponent does. I typically use mine early on non-optimal situations.
This gives me less blood, but if I beat my opponent to it he won't
be able to use it at all.

// Blood Doll cost nothing to play, but costs

// 2 vampire blood to get 1 pool. (This would
// equate Blood Doll with a Powerbase. The
// Blood Doll as it is, is equivalent to
// 2 Powerbases without a cost to play it.)

Not quite 2 Powerbases (ie. subtle difference -- this blood comes from

your vampires) , but the steal-ability given to power bases is enough

to counter the fact that your vampire gets weaker so I'll give you some

credit on the blood doll, hey, if I can put two blood on a vampire for

one pool (the reverse) this card is still reasonably cool. It's still
not terriblily game wrecking the way it is though.

// It's quite clear that the Ivory Bow's capabilities
// should well cost 2 pool (compare with Flamethrower),
// but it is designed to be a rare unique card that
// motivitates players to buy a lot of cards to
// get one, so I won't argue too much about
// the case here.

Comparing with the Flamethrower I see that a second point of aggravated
damage on a more common card is worth 3 pool. Both weapons cannot be
pumped to do more damage. The really important fact here is that the
ivory bow will never remove a blood from the opponent, it only sends
them to torpor. Now the Flamethrower, it always removes one blood from
the vampire, and when you've got KRCG and you start interfering with
vampires who are hunting with no blood, you get dead vampires. Not
undead... _real_ dead... these guys don't bounce back. Basically,

there is quite a good argument for the 2nd agg. damage being worth the
3 (besides even at 2 the ivory bow is still easily concealed and if
they remove the disipline symbol off the Conceal Weapon card... you're
going to see Mudded Vampire Hunters with it regardless of the upped
cost).

// Some general hosers such as Charnas the Imp is also

// IMO a bit cheesy. Unlike Muddled Vampire Hunter,
// which can easily be neutralized, there is no
// good counter for Charnas: the Tremere pays 1 blood,
// and the victim is pretty doomed. IMO either the action
// to get Charnas should be more blockable, such
// as (D) at +0 stealth, or Charnas should cost 2 or 3
// blood. Even if it costs 3 blood, a lot of
// people are still going to use it (because
// it's very difficult to get rid of, once hooked).

There are a few ways to deal with Charnas. If you're playing Tremere
you can contest (his key limitation is he's unique... so you can only

have one out at a time (this is good)). Another nasty way is to Fame

that vampire, get the little bastard to work for you. If your prey put
it on the vampire, he's more than ready to shoot it now (I know I
was).

// The most of all, Fame. Can easily make your

// prey lose 3 blood, sometimes repeatedly, without
// a cost to play the card.

Fame is a spoiler. It needs some serious fixing. I hope they
have/will re-reverse the fame cummulative ruling back to "nope, it's
not... just like Army of Rats". Removing the 3 pool loss from the
current would make it much more reasonable, but probably far too weak
to be a resonable card. I've seen Fame kill people both intentionally,
and not intentionally (well, less direct (predator doesn't really want
it, but someone else decides it's required)... a Famed vampire really
doesn't "not intentionally" go to torpor) far too many times for it to
be considered a well balanced card. Probably, fame would be better if

it allowed a +1 stealth directed action to "De-fame" the vampire. This
would allow the prey to get rid of it without having to have a sudden
reversal in hand the moment it was played. With this modification it
can probably keep the 3 pool loss bit.

Well, that's enough of a commentary... please forgive the spelling/grammer
errors in the above, I'm terriblily tired and need to grab a few hours
sleep before this afternoon.

